    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.672, 630.724, 485.329, 677.262 ],
    "font" : "URWPalladioL-Roma",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Throughout we assume the Riemann Hypothesis, in the strong form that the nontrivial zeros are also simple. We also need to assume the following:"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 3,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 126.672, 523.572, 485.328, 619.176 ],
    "font" : "URWPalladioL-Roma",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Hypothesis D. The level curves Re(η(s)) = 0 are differentiable. This is automatic except at isolated points where η′(s) = 0, so we are really assuming that when η′(s) = 0, arg(η(s)) ̸= ±π/2. This prevents the level curves from branching. Hypothesis D is plausible because ±π/2 are only two points on the unit circle, while the zeros of η′(s) form a countable set."
  }, {
